TI  - THE EFFECT OF VITAMIN-E AND DIETARY LINOLEIC-ACID ON MERCURY TOXICITY
T2  - NUTRITION REPORTS INTERNATIONAL
AB  - Japanese quail chicks were intoxicated with 25 ppm Hg as CH3HgCl or 500 ppm Hg as HgCl2. The addition of Se and vitamin E reduced the percent Hg-induced mortality. Both 25 ppm Hg as Ch3HgCl and 500 ppm HG as HgCl2 resulted in .apprx. 50% mortality by day 20 when given with 1 ppm Se and no added vitamin E. Vitamin E (50-500 mg/kg) was not as effective in reducing HgCl2-induced mortality as it was in reducing CH3HgCl-induced mortality. The incorporation at increasing levels (1-3%) of linoleic acid in the diet exacerbated the lethal effects of CH3HgCl in quail maintained on the linoleic acid diets 15 days prior to Hg administration. Increasing PUFA [polyunsaturated fatty acid] in the diet may increase the quail''s susceptibility to Hg poisoning.
