TI  - PLATELET-VESSEL WALL INTERACTION - INFLUENCE OF DIET
T2  - PHILOSOPHICAL TRANSACTIONS OF THE ROYAL SOCIETY B-BIOLOGICAL SCIENCES
AB  - The interaction of platelets with the vessel wall is influenced by the diet. Of major importance is the dietary polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFA). Epidemiological evidence from Greenland Eskimos with low incidences of acute myocardial infarction has drawn attention to the role of the n - 3 PUFA family. Experiments in vitro have demonstrated an anti-aggregative effect of eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA). EPA does not inhibit vascular prostacyclin production. Anti-aggregative substances generated from EPA have not yet been demonstrated in vivo. Studies in vivo in animals and humans demonstrated an antithrombotic effect of EPA. In a study where volunteers were given 6 g EPA/day for 3 wk, moderate decreases in collagen-induced and ADP-induced platelet aggregation, lower thromboxane B2 (TXB2) synthesis and prolongation of bleeding time were found. Evidently, dietary factors modulate the interaction of platelets and the vessel wall. Dietary advice aiming at lowering the incidence of ischemic diseases must include this aspect. This necessitates a reevaluation of advice hitherto given to the population in general.
