AU  - Siguel, E
A1  - AMER ACAD ENVIRONM MED
TI  - Essential fatty acids: Assessing their role in cardiovascular diseases and immune deficiencies
T2  - WORLD OUT OF BALANCE: THE MICROBIAL-POLLUTION CONNECTION: WAKE UP CALL: 31ST ANNUAL MEETING OF THE AMERICAN ACADEMY OF ENVIRONMENTAL MEDICINE
CP  - 31st Annual Meeting of the American-Academy-of-Environmental-Medicine on World Out of Balance - The Microbial-Pollution Connection - Wake Up Call
AB  - Essential Fatty Acids (EFAs) have been known to be essential nutrients for more than 50 years. Diagnosis of EFA abnormalities took a gigantic step with the advent of commercial gas liquid chromatography (GLC) in the 1950's, but had to wait for high resolution capillary GLC in the 1980's to achieve its full potential. Initially, only the most severe cases of EFA deficiency (EFAD) could be diagnosed; equivalent to detecting cholesterol over 4,000 mg/dl or highway speeders going at 1,200 miles/hour. One of the most commonly used measures of EFAD is the ratio 20:3 omega 9/20:4 omega 6 -- the ratio of the concentrations of triene (20:3 omega 9) to tetraene (20:4 omega 6) (T/T). Initially a ratio of T/T > 0.4 was proposed as a marker of EFAD. It was then lowered to 0.2 (the value still stated in most books and journals). These ranges are suitable to detect severe EFAD in animals and a few rare patients with severe GI malabsorption or on diets severely restricted on EFAs. Siguel lowered the upper normal limit of T/T to 0.025.(1) This 10x increase in sensitivity allows us to identify EFAD in patients with cardiovascular disease and immune disorders, characterize abnormal EFA metabolism, and study the effects of different food supplements on EFA metabolism. More than 25% of the adult American population has low levels of omega 3 or omega 6. Patients with heart disease, or at high risk for heart disease as indicated by elevated Total/HDL Cholesterol ratios (TC/HDLC), have biochemical evidence of EFAD. Patients with a wide range of conditions have inappropriate production of EFA metabolites; modulation of EFA metabolites with diet provides a highly powerful tool to treat a wide range of disorders, including complications of diabetes mellitus, cystic fibrosis, Crohn's disease, dermatitis, CFS, eye disorders, brain dysfunction, behavioral disturbances, depression, and neurological diseases. Most patients need supplements of omega 3 and omega 6s, often with more omega 3s to correct a proportionately greater deficiency in omega 3 than omega 6s. Some patients need EFA derivatives such as GLA, EPA and DHA; in these cases, it is important to monitor PUFAs carefully in order to achieve the critical balance.
