TI  - Flaxseed and flaxseed products for cattle: Effects on health, growth performance, carcass quality, and sensory attributes
T2  - PROCEEDINGS OF THE 59TH FLAX INSTITUTE OF THE UNITED STATES
CP  - 59th Flax-Institute-of-the-United-States
AB  - Two experiments were conducted with weaned calves to evaluate the effects of feeding flaxseed and flaxseed products on morbidity rates, mortality rates, and growth performance. In Trial 1, steer calves (n=370 steers, 463 lb BW) were fed diets containing flaxseed at 0, 5, 10, 15, or 20%, or tallow at 4% of the diet. In Trial 2, heifer calves (n=365 heifers, 474 lb BW) were fed diets containing no added fat, 4% beef tallow, 10% ground flaxseed, 4% flaxseed oil, or a combination of linseed meal with 4% added tallow. In Trial 1, feed intake responded quadratically (P<0.04) to flax addition, with 10% flax yielding maximum feed intake, maximum gain, and optimum feed efficiency. Feeding flaxseed increased circulating concentrations of omega-3 fatty acids, including 18:3, 20:3, 22:3, 22:5, and 22:6 fatty acids. Compared to cattle fed tallow, cattle fed 10% flax (approximately isocaloric diets) tended to be more efficient. Incidence of respiratory disease was not different among dietary treatments. In Trial 2, feeding flaxseed or flaxseed oil resulted in greater feed intakes (P<.01) and greater weight gains (P<0.01) during the first 8 days after arrival compared to cattle fed tallow. Incidence of bovine respiratory disease and death loss were decreased (P=0.09 and P=0.01, respectively) by including any form of supplemental fat in the diet. Compared to diets containing tallow, diets that included flaxseed or flaxseed oil resulted in higher circulating concentrations of omega-3 fatty acids and improved (P=0.06) response to antibiotic therapy in animals treated for bovine respiratory disease. Including flaxseed or flaxseed oil in the diet during the initial 5 to 6 weeks generally increased fat deposition during the subsequent finishing phase, resulting in substantial improvements in carcass quality grade when cattle were slaughtered in excess of 150 days after removal of flax products from the diet. In general, flaxseed oil (provide as refined oil or as ground flaxseed) is superior to tallow as a source of lipid in the diets of stressed feeder cattle, resulting in improved health and carcass quality.
