AU  - ALEXANDER, JW
TI  - ALTERED MACROPHAGE FUNCTION IN DIETARY IMMUNOREGULATION
T2  - JOURNAL OF PARENTERAL AND ENTERAL NUTRITION
AB  - It is well established that nutrition can profoundly influence immunologic function. An important component of nutritional intervention in immune regulation is dietary lipid intake. Recent studies have implicated that dietary lipids are important in regulating the immune response to neoplasms, autoimmune disease states, infection, trauma, and transplanted organs. Studies from our laboratory have focused on the effect of different lipids on allograft survival in a rat heart transplant model. We have shown the dietary supplementation with the polyunsaturated fatty acid, linoleic acid, prolongs allograft survival and when given in combination with a donor specific blood transfusion acts synergistically. Conversely, oleic acid, a monounsaturated fatty acid, shortened allograft survival in transfused animals. It has been postulated that the mechanism of this lipid effect on immunity is via alterations in cell membrane fatty acid composition which may effect recognition or presentation of antigens. It is thus possible that the macrophage which has a central role in antigen presentation and processing would be effected by dietary lipids. Herein, the effect of different dietary lipids on macrophage function is studied. Also, dietary-induced alterations in serum fatty acid composition are measured to determine a possible relationship between fatty acid metabolites and the immune  response.
