TI  - Mitochondrial membrane alterations by aging and diet: Impact on recovery of myocardial function after stress
T2  - ADAPTATION BIOLOGY AND MEDICINE, VOL 3: NEW FRONTIERS
CP  - 6th World Congress of the International-Society-for-Adaptive-Medicine
AB  - Compared to hearts from younger counterparts, the senescent myocardium has a reduced capacity to recover from aerobic or ischemic stress and exhibits a reduced Ca2+ tolerance during increased Ca2+ influx due to augmented cardiac work, post-ischemic reflow or oxidative stress. Brief stress that perturbs Ca2+ homeostasis is usually managed in part by mitochondrial buffering of Ca2+ but prolonged stress leads to loss of ionic and metabolic homeostasis, accumulation of toxic metabolite and peroxidation products, leading to cell injury and arrhythmias. Myocardial cell membrane "remodeling" is a major factor underlying the relative Ca2+ intolerance in senescence. In addition to cell loss, cells increase in size and exhibit altered gene expression of key proteins, including those that sustain Ca2+ homeostasis. Major membrane changes in aging include increases in membrane rigidity, cholesterol, phosphatidylcholine, omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFA), 4-hydroxy-2 nonenal, and decreases in omega-3 PUFA, mitochondrial cardiolipin, and antioxidants such as coenzyme Q(10) (CoQ(10)). These alterations have profound consequences on the efficacy of membrane proteins involved with ion homeostasis, signal transduction, redox reactions and oxidative phosphorylation. Compared to young hearts, mitochondria in aged hearts have a greater propensity for augmented generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) with negative consequences that are compounded by reduced levels of SOD and CoQ(10). Notably, some of the age-related adaptations described above can be beneficially modified by dietary strategy. Diet rich in omega-3 PUFA reverses the age-associated membrane omega-6: omega-3 PUFA imbalance, attenuates mitochondrial phospholipid modification and Ca2+ homeostasis, and facilitates increased efficiency of energy production and improved tolerance of ischemia-reperfusion. CoQ(10), from diet or in vitro treatment, reverses the age-associated decline in mitochondrial CoQ(10) levels and augments the efficiency of ADP:O consumption. Direct CoQ(10) treatment of senescent myocardial cells in vitro results in reduced susceptibility to ROS-induction of the mitochondrial permeability transition pore and cell death. CoQ(10) treatment of senescent isolated working rat hearts reverses age-associated reduction in O-2- Utilisation efficiency during high work aerobic pacing stress. CoQ(10) treatment of senescent human atrial trabeculae increases post-ischemic recovery of contractile function. Thus, dietary modification of cardiac membrane lipid and antioxidant content may have a profound impact on age-associated myocardial adaptation and response to ischemia-reperfusion injury.
