TI  - Avoiding Parenteral Nutrition associated Cholestasis in Short Bowel Syndrome might reduce the need of a combined hepatic-bowel transplantation
T2  - XI INTERNATIONAL SMALL BOWEL TRANSPLANT SYMPOSIUM
CP  - 11th International Symposium on Small Bowel Transplant
AB  - Intestinal rehabilitation can be reached in short bowel syndrome with a strategy that merges nutritional pharmacologic and surgical approaches. Long-term parenteral nutrition required for the adaptation process is associated with complications, most of which have received technical responses. Parenteral nutrition associated cholestasis and parenteral nutrition associated liver disease remain indeed the most worrisome complications and bear with them a high mortality rate. Virtually all patients who undergo combined liver and small-bowel transplantation suffer from end-stage liver disease, and a great many recipients of isolated intestinal transplantation also have some degree of liver dysfunction. This article will illustrates our experience on the role of n-3 fatty acids in the prevention and cure of liver dysfunction.
